ga5F(){return"On the Beat or Off?"},
gatT(){return"Straight rhythm, or syncopated?"},
gazw(){return"Is this rhythm on the beat, or syncopated?"},
gazx(){return"On the beat"},
gazy(){return"Syncopated"},
ga5O(){return"Even or Triplet?"},
gau1(){return"Is the beat split in two, or in three?"},
gbt8(){return"How is the beat split?"},
gbt7(){return"Even (2)"},
gbt9(){return"Triplet (3)"},
ga57(){return"Which Ornament?"},
gatw(){return"Read the trill, mordent or turn"},
gbeQ(){return"Which ornament is on the note?"},
gbeR(){return"Trill"},
gbeP(){return"Mordent"},
gbeS(){return"Turn"},
gbj8(){return"On the beat, or off?"},
gbj7(){return"Usually the notes land right ON the beats \u2014 count 1-2-3-4 and each note lands on a number. Steady and square."},
gbj6(){return'Syncopation pushes notes OFF the beat, onto the "and" in between. The accent lands where your ear didnt expect it \u2014 thats the kick you feel in pop and jazz.'},
gbju(){return"Two or three in a beat"},
gbjs(){return'Normally a beat splits into TWO even halves: "1-and". Two eighth notes.'},
gbjt(){return'A triplet squeezes THREE equal notes into that same beat: "trip-o-let". It gets a little 3 above it.'},
gbip(){return"Decorating a note"},
gbiq(){return"Ornaments are little signs that dress up a note. A trill (tr) shakes quickly between the note and the one just above it."},
gbir(){return"A turn (a sideways S) curls AROUND the note: the note above, the note, the note below, then back. A mordent is just one quick flick up and back."},
ga4r(){return"Label the Form"},
gasV(){return"Hear the sections; name the shape (ABA\u2026)"},
gb3z(){return"What is the form? (same colour = same tune)"},
gb3y(){return"Listen"},
gbhs(){return"The shape of a piece"},
gbhr(){return"Music is built from sections. Here is a little tune \u2014 call it section A. Whenever it comes back, it is A again."},
gbhq(){return"A different tune is a new letter \u2014 section B. Tune, different tune, then the first tune again makes the form A-B-A. Lots of songs are shaped this way!"},
ga4T(){return"Which Mode?"},
gatm(){return"Major, minor or Dorian?"},
gbcG(){return"Listen! Which mode is it?"},
ga1l(){return"Major"},
ga1m(){return"Minor"},
gbcF(){return"Dorian"},
gbil(){return"Three colours of scale"},
gbij(){return"A major scale sounds bright and happy. Listen to it climb."},
gbik(){return"A minor scale sounds darker \u2014 its 3rd, 6th and 7th steps sit a little lower."},
gbii(){return"Dorian is like minor, but its 6th step is raised \u2014 so it sounds minor with a brighter twist. That one note is the whole secret!"},
gaoX(){return"Textbook"},
gbpe(){return"Read"},
gbpb(){return"Work through music from the very start. Each topic has a short lesson (see it, hear it) and games to practise it."},
